    "essay": "The book of Genesis places in the midst of the garden of Eden a tree whose fruit is life (Genesis 2:9). Beside it stands the tree of the knowledge of good and evil. Man is free to eat of every tree save one. The tree of life is not introduced as a decorative myth. It belongs to the truth that the human creature was made for something more than biological survival: communion with the living God in a state in which death was not the natural horizon of flesh taken from the dust and breathed upon by the divine Spirit.\n\nWhen the man and the woman have taken from the forbidden tree, the Lord God says, “Behold Adam is become as one of us, knowing good and evil: now, therefore, lest perhaps he put forth his hand, and take also of the tree of life, and eat, and live for ever” (Genesis 3:22). Cherubim and a flaming sword are set to keep the way. The first exile in Scripture is not only a change of address. It is exclusion from a gift. Access to life is barred because life seized in a state of rebellion would not be blessedness; it would be the eternalization of the wound.\n\nCatholic theology has never treated this chapter as ornamental folklore. The Catechism of the Catholic Church teaches that man was created in a state of original holiness and justice, and that death entered the world as a consequence of sin (cf. Romans 5:12; Wisdom 2:23–24). The tree of life therefore names, first, what humanity was made for; second, why that end cannot be grasped on human terms after the fall; and third—when the canon is read as a whole—how God himself restores access.\n\nThe Fathers read the Cross as the answer to the barred garden. St. Irenaeus of Lyons, in the fifth book of *Against Heresies*, insists upon recapitulation: the Lord suffered on the tree so that by the tree he might undo the disobedience that came through a tree. The symmetry is deliberate. Where the first Adam stretched out his hand to seize, the second Adam stretches out his hands upon the wood to give. The drama of Genesis is not discarded; it is taken up and healed. St. Augustine preaches the same conversion of images: the Cross becomes the tree of life brought into the place of our exile, because we could not storm paradise by our own strength. Access is restored not by climbing the wall but by a death that destroys death.\n\nThis reading is not a medieval invention. It is the ordinary instinct of the Church when she contemplates the Passion as the center of the world’s history. The liturgy of the Cross, the sign of the cross traced on the body, the wood kissed on Good Friday—all of these gestures assume that the instrument of shame has become the instrument of life. The tree that killed has become the tree that heals.\n\nThe canonical arc confirms the instinct. In the Apocalypse the risen Christ promises the conqueror “to eat of the tree of life, which is in the paradise of my God” (Revelation 2:7). In the final vision a river of the water of life proceeds from the throne of God and of the Lamb, and on either side stands the tree of life, yielding its fruit and bearing leaves “for the healing of the nations” (Revelation 22:1–2). The sword is gone. The nations are not healed by nostalgia for Eden but by the Lamb who was slain. Between the locked gate of Genesis and the open city of Revelation stands the whole economy of redemption—and, within that economy, the sacramental life of the Church.\n\nFor the Eucharist is not a late ritual curiosity. If the Cross is the true tree of life planted in history, then the Body of Christ given as food is the fruit of that tree. The Church already tastes, under the veil of signs, the life that the last pages of Scripture display without veil. The tree of life thus teaches the doctrine of original holiness, the gravity of the fall, the necessity of a Redeemer, the meaning of the Passion, and the hope of glory—not as disconnected theses, but as one story with one wood at its center.\n\nThe theological weight of this image appears again whenever the Church speaks of eternal life. Eternal life is not mere endless duration; it is the life of God shared with the creature. The tree in the garden was already a sacrament of that gift in the order of original holiness. The Cross is the sacrament of that gift in the order of redemption. The vision of Revelation is that gift without veil. Catholic eschatology is therefore not an escape from history but the healing of the nations by the leaves of the tree that grows from the throne of the Lamb.\n\nPastoral consequence follows. A Christian who makes the sign of the cross is already confessing that the wood of shame is the wood of life. A Christian who approaches the altar is already reaching for the fruit that Adam could not seize. A Christian who hopes for the dead is already trusting that the sword no longer has the last word at the gate.",
