    "essay": "Genesis 3 narrates the entrance of sin into human history with a severity that later theology has never been able to soften without loss. The serpent speaks. The woman and the man take and eat. Their eyes are opened not to divine wisdom but to shame. They hide among the trees. Called by the Lord, they shift blame. The ground is cursed. Pain and toil enter the horizon of human life. Death is announced. They are clothed by God himself and driven from the garden. Cherubim guard the way to the tree of life.\n\nScripture is not describing a private embarrassment in a mythic park. It is describing the rupture of the relationship for which humanity was made. The heart of the act is distrust: the suspicion that God withholds what is good, that the creature must seize what the Creator has not given. From that distrust flow fear, division, accusation, and the long human habit of reaching for life as if it had to be stolen.\n\nSt. Paul draws the doctrinal line with apostolic authority. “Wherefore as by one man sin entered into this world, and by sin death; and so death passed upon all men, in whom all have sinned” (Romans 5:12). He does not stop at diagnosis. He sets Adam and Christ as two heads of the race: “For as by the disobedience of one man, many were made sinners; so also by the obedience of one, many shall be made just” (Romans 5:19). If Adam is only a bad example, Christ is only a better example. Paul preaches a real solidarity in ruin answered by a real solidarity in redemption. “As in Adam all die, so also in Christ all shall be made alive” (1 Corinthians 15:22).\n\nThe Church defined this teaching against every reduction that would make grace optional. The Council of Trent, in the fifth session, anathematized the claim that Adam’s sin injured himself alone and not his posterity, and the claim that he transmitted only death and bodily pains, not sin which is the death of the soul. Original sin is the privation of original holiness and justice. It is transmitted by propagation, not by imitation alone. Baptism remits its guilt. Concupiscence remains in the baptized as a wound and an inclination to struggle against, not as guilt in itself. These distinctions are not scholastic hair-splitting; they are the Church’s way of telling the truth both about the depth of the fall and about the efficacy of the sacrament.\n\nSt. Augustine, contending with Pelagius, refused any account of human nature in which the will could raise itself by advice and effort alone. “No one is freed from the body of this death except by the grace of the Redeemer.” The Pelagian path looked merciful because it flattered human strength; it was cruel because it left the wounded without a physician. St. Irenaeus, a century and a half earlier, had already framed the same history as recapitulation: the story broken in Adam is taken up in Christ and lived through to obedience, so that the human race is not abandoned to its first chapter.\n\nGenesis 3, however, is not only autopsy. Before exile is complete, God addresses the serpent with a promise of enmity between the serpent and the woman, between his seed and her seed (Genesis 3:15). The tradition names this the *protoevangelium*—the first gospel. Judgment is real; despair is not authorized. The need for a Savior is universal; the promise of a Savior is already on the table. Baptism, the Cross, and the whole life of grace answer this chapter. If they do not, they answer nothing that finally matters.\n\nThe pastoral and sacramental consequences are immediate. Infant baptism is not an insult to the child’s innocence of personal acts; it is the Church’s obedience to the width of the wound and the width of the cure. The confessional is not a booth of scrupulosity; it is the place where actual sins, which grow in the soil of the inherited wound, are judged and forgiven. Ascetic struggle against concupiscence is not dualist hatred of the body; it is warfare for the integration of a nature that is good as created and wounded as fallen.\n\nModern objections often ask whether such a doctrine is compatible with human dignity. The Catholic answer is that dignity is precisely why the wound matters. If the human being were a trivial animal, the fall would be trivial. Because the human being is made for God, the loss of original holiness is catastrophic—and the Incarnation is proportionate to the catastrophe. A small diagnosis yields a small Christ. Trent refused a small diagnosis.",
