    "essay": "In the act of judging the serpent, God speaks a word that the Church has never been able to hear as mere curse. “I will put enmities between thee and the woman, and thy seed and her seed: she shall crush thy head, and thou shalt lie in wait for her heel” (Genesis 3:15, in the traditional Catholic reading). In the middle of the wreckage of the fall, Scripture announces a war that the serpent will not win. Later ages named this verse the *protoevangelium*—the first gospel—because the good news of a victor is already present where the bad news of disobedience is being pronounced.\n\nThe New Testament does not quote the verse as a proof-text in isolation, but the whole shape of the Gospel—the woman, the child, the conflict with the devil, the victory through suffering—fills what Genesis begins. St. Justin Martyr, in the *Dialogue with Trypho*, sets Eve and Mary in deliberate contrast: Eve, still a virgin, receives the word of the serpent and brings forth disobedience and death; Mary receives the word of the angel in faith and joy. St. Irenaeus of Lyons states the classic form of the parallel in *Against Heresies*: the knot of Eve’s disobedience is loosed by the obedience of Mary; what the virgin Eve bound through unbelief, the Virgin Mary looses through faith. The point is not to construct a cult of Mary independent of Christ. The point is that God heals the human story from within the human story—through a woman’s consent and through the seed who is Christ.\n\nThe “seed of the woman” is fulfilled in the Incarnation and the Passion. The Cross is the crushing of the serpent’s head; the Resurrection is the public proof that the bite was not final. Marian doctrine grows from this soil without replacing the Redeemer as the center. She belongs to the enmity and to the victory as the New Eve, always subordinate to the New Adam. The Church’s liturgical instinct—her willingness to sing of Mary where the serpent is put to flight—rests on this early patristic reading of Genesis.\n\nRead this way, Genesis 3:15 is not a footnote. It is the spine of Scripture’s conflict narrative: the serpent’s line and the promise’s line, until Golgotha and the empty tomb. Every later prophecy of a savior-king, every type of a victorious lamb, every cry for deliverance from evil, moves inside the enmity God himself declared.\n\nIn the wider life of the Church this moment is not left on the page. It is proclaimed in the liturgy, interpreted by the Fathers, defined where necessary by the councils, and lived in the sacraments. Scripture alone, read apart from the Church that received it, is not the Catholic rule; yet Scripture, read in the Church, is never optional. The pivotal moments of the Bible are pivotal precisely because the Spirit who inspired the text is the same Spirit who builds the Body. To linger here is not antiquarianism. It is obedience to the way God has chosen to reveal and to save.\n\nAmong the Latin Fathers, the instinct is the same as among the Greeks: these events are not raw material for private allegory, but public deeds of God interpreted by the Church’s rule of faith. St. Jerome’s insistence on returning to the Hebrew and Greek does not cancel typology; it disciplines it. St. Ambrose’s sacramental mystagogy does not float free of history; it binds the catechumen to the acts God has already performed. St. Gregory the Great’s moral reading does not reduce the text to tips for self-improvement; it asks what form of life answers the gift.\n\nThe medieval doctors received this inheritance and ordered it. St. Thomas distinguishes the literal sense from the spiritual senses without allowing the literal to be discarded. What God did in Israel, he did in truth; what he signified for the Church, he signified in truth. The spiritual sense is not a second Bible invented by cleverness; it is the same Spirit disclosing the same Christ.\n\nIn modern controversy the Catholic position remains steady. Historical study of the text is welcome; the amputation of typology and dogma is not. The pivotal moment is pivotal because the economy of salvation turns upon it—not because a later age finds it psychologically interesting. Read with the Fathers, the councils, and the liturgy, the page becomes what it was always meant to be: a place where the living God addresses the living Church.\n",
