    "essay": "The Lord said to Abram: “Go out of thy country, and from thy kindred, and out of thy father’s house, and come into the land which I shall shew thee. And I will make of thee a great nation, and I will bless thee, and magnify thy name, and thou shalt be blessed. I will bless them that bless thee, and curse them that curse thee, and in thee shall all the kindred of the earth be blessed” (Genesis 12:1–3). The story of Israel — and of the Church’s claim to be the family of the promise — begins with a command to leave and a promise that reaches the nations.\n\nIn Genesis 15 Abraham believes the Lord, and it is “reputed to him unto justice” (Genesis 15:6). Paul will return to this verse as the pattern of justification by faith (Romans 4; Galatians 3). The covenant is cut in the night of divided animals; a smoking furnace and a burning lamp pass between the pieces while Abraham sleeps — God binding himself when the human partner is passive (Genesis 15:17–18). In Genesis 17 the covenant is marked in the flesh by circumcision; the names change; Sarah will bear the heir; kings shall come from them; God will be their God.\n\nThe binding of Isaac (Genesis 22) tests the promise at its root: the son of the promise is the one demanded, and the Lord provides the ram. Hebrews and James read the episode as faith perfected in works and as hope in God’s power to raise the dead. The whole Abraham cycle is therefore both historical election and permanent school of Christian faith — leave, trust, believe against the evidence of the body, receive the heir, offer him back, receive him again in figure.\n\nCatholic reading refuses to sever Abraham from Christ. The blessing of the nations is not a footnote; it is the telos. Baptism grafts the nations into the faith of Abraham; the Eucharist is the better sacrifice toward which the ram on Moriah pointed; the Church is the multitude of stars and sand. The particularity of Israel is not cancelled by universality; universality arrives through the particular seed, who is Christ (Galatians 3:16). The pivotal moment is the beginning of the people of God under promise — a beginning the Gospel does not abandon but fulfills.",
