    "essay": "“O God my God, look upon me: why hast thou forsaken me?” (Psalm 22:1). The psalm that opens in desolation describes the mockery of enemies, the piercing of hands and feet, the dividing of garments by lot, and then turns to a vow of praise that reaches “all the ends of the earth” (Psalm 22:16–27). Jesus prays the opening cry from the Cross (Matthew 27:46). The evangelists mark the division of garments as fulfillment (John 19:23–24).\n\nSt. Justin Martyr, in the *Dialogue with Trypho*, argues from these lines to the crucified Messiah. The Church’s Holy Week still inhabits this psalm because it tells the truth about suffering without lying about trust, and because the Passion is ordered toward the conversion of the nations. The lament of the righteous sufferer becomes the prayer of the crucified Lord and the hope of the world.\n\nIn the wider life of the Church this moment is not left on the page. It is proclaimed in the liturgy, interpreted by the Fathers, defined where necessary by the councils, and lived in the sacraments. Scripture alone, read apart from the Church that received it, is not the Catholic rule; yet Scripture, read in the Church, is never optional. The pivotal moments of the Bible are pivotal precisely because the Spirit who inspired the text is the same Spirit who builds the Body. To linger here is not antiquarianism. It is obedience to the way God has chosen to reveal and to save.\n\n\nThe reader is invited to return from this page to the biblical text itself—with the Fathers at the elbow and the liturgy in the bones. The Church does not ask for curiosity alone. She asks for the obedience of faith that lets these events judge and heal the life of the one who reads. What God has done, he has done once; what he continues to give in Word and Sacrament is the same gift, present to this hour.\n\nAmong the Latin Fathers, the instinct is the same as among the Greeks: these events are not raw material for private allegory, but public deeds of God interpreted by the Church’s rule of faith. St. Jerome’s insistence on returning to the Hebrew and Greek does not cancel typology; it disciplines it. St. Ambrose’s sacramental mystagogy does not float free of history; it binds the catechumen to the acts God has already performed. St. Gregory the Great’s moral reading does not reduce the text to tips for self-improvement; it asks what form of life answers the gift.\n\nThe medieval doctors received this inheritance and ordered it. St. Thomas distinguishes the literal sense from the spiritual senses without allowing the literal to be discarded. What God did in Israel, he did in truth; what he signified for the Church, he signified in truth. The spiritual sense is not a second Bible invented by cleverness; it is the same Spirit disclosing the same Christ.\n\nIn modern controversy the Catholic position remains steady. Historical study of the text is welcome; the amputation of typology and dogma is not. The pivotal moment is pivotal because the economy of salvation turns upon it—not because a later age finds it psychologically interesting. Read with the Fathers, the councils, and the liturgy, the page becomes what it was always meant to be: a place where the living God addresses the living Church.\n",
