    "essay": "Jerusalem falls. The Temple is burned. The king is blinded and led away. “By the rivers of Babylon, there we sat and wept: when we remembered Sion” (Psalm 137:1). The exile is not only geopolitical disaster; it is theological crisis. The land of promise is lost; the house where the Name dwelt is ash; the Davidic throne seems broken.\n\nYet the prophets refuse despair. Jeremiah writes to the exiles to build houses and seek the peace of the city, and he promises a future and a hope after seventy years (Jeremiah 29). Ezekiel sees a valley of dry bones and a heart of stone replaced by a heart of flesh (Ezekiel 36–37). The covenant is not cancelled because the people are judged; judgment is the severe mercy that prepares restoration.\n\nFor Catholic faith the exile trains the Church in two truths. First, sacred institutions — even the Temple — are not idols; God can withdraw the sign when the reality is despised. Second, God goes with his people into the furnace. Synagogue and Scripture intensify in exile; identity is no longer only geography but memory, law, and hope. When the remnant returns and the Second Temple rises, the stage is set for a greater presence: not only a house of stone, but the Word made flesh who will say that something greater than the Temple is here.\n\nThe exile is therefore a pivotal hinge between monarchy and messiah, between the old cult and the longing for a new covenant written on the heart. Without it, the gravity of Israel’s sin and the tenacity of God’s promise are both understated.\n",
