    "essay": "Jesus comes from Galilee to the Jordan to be baptized by John. The Baptist hesitates: “I ought to be baptized by thee, and comest thou to me?” Jesus answers: “Suffer it to be so now. For so it becometh us to fulfill all justice” (Matthew 3:14–15). The sinless one stands in the river of repentance — not to confess personal fault, but to stand with sinners and to sanctify the waters.\n\nWhen Jesus is baptized, the heavens open, the Spirit descends like a dove, and the Father’s voice declares: “This is my beloved Son, in whom I am well pleased” (Matthew 3:16–17). The scene is a theophany of the Trinity at the threshold of the public ministry. Catholic liturgy keeps this mystery at Epiphany’s wider light and at every baptismal font: what happened in the Jordan is the pattern of Christian initiation — water, Spirit, and the adopted sonship of those reborn in Christ.\n\nThe Fathers insist that Christ did not need baptism; the waters needed Christ. By entering them he purified what he would later command the Church to use. The Flood judged the world by water; the Red Sea drowned Pharaoh and saved Israel; the Jordan now becomes the place where the new Joshua (Jesus) begins the conquest of the true inheritance. The pivotal moment joins Old Testament water-types to the sacrament of baptism and reveals the Trinity’s delight in the Son who will go from the river to the Cross.\n",
