    "essay": "Forty days after the Resurrection, Jesus leads the disciples out to Bethany, lifts his hands, and is carried up into heaven (Luke 24:50–51; Acts 1:9). A cloud receives him; two men in white announce that he will return as he was seen to go. The Ascension is not an escape from the body. It is the enthronement of the crucified and risen humanity of Christ at the right hand of the Father.\n\nCatholic dogma holds that the same Jesus who suffered under Pontius Pilate now reigns in glory, still true God and true man. The Ascension means that human nature — assumed in the womb of Mary, offered on the Cross, raised on the third day — has been taken into the inner life of God. Where the Head has gone, the Body is called to follow. The letter to the Ephesians speaks of Christ ascending on high and giving gifts to men (Ephesians 4:8–10); Pentecost will be the first great outpouring of those gifts.\n\nFor the Church’s mission the Ascension is pivotal: the visible presence withdraws so that faith may mature and the Spirit may be given. The disciples return to Jerusalem with great joy and are found continually in the Temple praising God (Luke 24:52–53). Absence is not abandonment. The Lord remains present in Word, sacrament, and the poor, until he comes again in glory. Every Mass already participates in the heavenly liturgy where the ascended Christ intercedes.\n",
