    "essay": "After the exile, Zechariah sees night visions and speaks words of rebuilding, cleansing, and future joy. Among them stands an oracle of the king: “Rejoice greatly, O daughter of Sion, shout for joy, O daughter of Jerusalem: Behold thy king will come to thee, the just and saviour: he is poor, and riding upon an ass, and upon a colt the foal of an ass” (Zechariah 9:9). The entry is royal and deliberately anti-imperial. Not chariots and warhorses — those are cut off in the same chapter — but a beast of burden, the mount of the poor.\n\nThe evangelists are explicit. Jesus sends for the donkey; the crowds spread garments and branches; Matthew and John cite Zechariah as fulfilled (Matthew 21:4–5; John 12:14–15). Palm Sunday is not the Church inventing a pageant; it is the Church watching Scripture walk into Jerusalem. The king is just and saving, yet poor. Within a week the same city will cry for crucifixion. The prophecy does not cancel the Cross; it frames it. The humble entry is the shape of the kingship that will be lifted up on the wood.\n\nPatristic and liturgical reading press the moral and dogmatic point. Messianic power is not Caesar’s. Peace to the nations (Zechariah 9:10) comes from a different kind of dominion — the dominion of the one who empties himself. Philippians 2 is the apostolic commentary: exaltation follows the form of a servant. Zechariah’s donkey is already that form in motion.\n\nThe pivotal moment trains the eye for Holy Week. If the king matches the prophecy only in spectacle and not in poverty, he is not Zechariah’s king. If the Church seeks triumph without the donkey’s humility, she has not yet understood her Lord’s entry. Rejoicing and poverty belong together until the kingdom is handed over to the Father.\n\nZechariah 9 continues: he shall speak peace to the Gentiles, and his power shall be from sea to sea (Zechariah 9:10). Palm Sunday’s joy is ordered to that universal peace, not to a local nationalist flare. The Church’s mission to the nations is the long echo of the donkey’s entry. When Christians seek influence by the world’s warhorses — prestige, coercion, spectacle without the Cross — they betray the prophecy they celebrate with palms. The pivotal moment is a permanent examination of conscience for Christian politics and Christian worship alike: is our king the one who comes poor, or have we requested another?",
