    "essay": "Zechariah 12 places Jerusalem under siege and under promise. The Lord will make Jerusalem a cup of stupor to the peoples; he will protect the tents of Judah; then comes the oracle of mourning and grace: “And I will pour out upon the house of David, and upon the inhabitants of Jerusalem, the spirit of grace, and of prayers: and they shall look upon me, whom they have pierced: and they shall mourn for him as one mourneth for an only son, and they shall grieve over him as the manner is to grieve for the death of the firstborn” (Zechariah 12:10). The “me” and the “him” intertwine. The pierced one is spoken of with the Lord’s own first person.\n\nJohn’s Gospel stands beneath the Cross when a soldier opens Jesus’ side with a spear. Blood and water flow at once. John testifies that he saw it, and he cites the Scripture: they shall look on him whom they pierced (John 19:34–37). The Church has never treated this as a minor medical detail. From the opened side the Fathers hear the birth of the Church, as Eve from Adam’s side; they hear the sacraments of baptism and Eucharist; they hear the Sacred Heart. Revelation returns to the theme: every eye shall see him, and they that pierced him (Revelation 1:7).\n\nChristologically the verse is among the most concentrated in the prophets. Looking, piercing, mourning, only son, firstborn, spirit of grace — the Passion is already structured here. The pivot is recognition: grace is poured out so that the gaze upon the wounded one becomes prayer rather than only guilt. Calvary is not merely a judicial execution; it is the place where Zechariah’s oracle becomes visible history.\n\nFor devotion, the lance and the open side are invitations to the same look. The pivotal moment forbids a Christianity that celebrates resurrection while refusing to contemplate the wound. The risen Christ still bears scars; the prophet already taught the Church to mourn and to receive the spirit of grace in one act of beholding.\n\nThe mourning “as for an only son” also gathers Israel and the Church into a shared grief that becomes grace. Romans 11 hopes for a mystery of grafting and mercy; Zechariah 12 already pours out a spirit of grace and of prayers upon those who look. Contemplation of the Passion is not morbidity; it is the prophet’s appointed path to the Spirit. The Sacred Heart devotion, the Good Friday liturgy, and the elevation of the Host are practices of the same look. The pivotal moment will not be finished until every eye sees him — and until mourning has been transfigured into the prayer of those who know they are loved by the one they pierced.",
