    "essay": "Daniel’s night visions rise through beastly kingdoms to a throne room. The Ancient of days sits; books are opened; then: “I beheld therefore in the vision of the night, and lo, one like the son of man came with the clouds of heaven, and he came even to the Ancient of days: and they presented him before him. And he gave him power, and glory, and a kingdom: and all peoples, tribes and tongues shall serve him: his power is an everlasting power that shall not be taken away: and his kingdom that shall not be destroyed” (Daniel 7:13–14).\n\n“Son of man” can mean simply a human figure over against the beasts. In Daniel’s vision that human figure receives the worship of the nations and everlasting dominion. Jesus takes the title as his characteristic self-name. Before the Sanhedrin, when charged under oath, he joins Psalm 110 and Daniel 7: you shall see the Son of man sitting on the right hand of the power of God and coming in the clouds of heaven (Matthew 26:64; Mark 14:62). The claim costs him the verdict of blasphemy. He is not dabbling in pious poetry; he is identifying himself with the cloud-borne heir of the kingdom.\n\nThe Gospels hold together the Son of Man’s authority and his suffering — the one who has nowhere to lay his head, who gives his life as a ransom, who judges the nations. Catholic faith reads Daniel 7 as already sketching Ascension and Parousia: the one who goes to the Ancient of days will return as he was seen to go. The title guards the mystery of the Incarnation: true man, yet more than one more victim of empire; the recipient of a kingdom that outlasts every beast.\n\nThe pivotal moment gives the Church her language in crisis. When powers beast-like crush the saints, Daniel’s vision answers: the last word is not the beast, but the Son of man and the everlasting kingdom. Christology here is hope under persecution, not only speculation in a chair.\n\nIn the liturgy of the Church, Daniel’s vision supports the confession that Christ will come again in glory to judge the living and the dead. The everlasting kingdom is not a metaphor for moral progress; it is the reign of the Son to whom the Father has given all judgment. Between the ascension and the return, the saints share in the resistance of the “holy people” against the beasts. Daniel 7 is therefore apocalyptic pastoral care: it names the monsters honestly and refuses them the last word. The Son of man has received the kingdom; the Church’s task is fidelity until the clouds that took him up bring him back.",
