    "essay": "Malachi stands at the end of the Twelve. Worship is corrupt; the covenant is profaned; yet the Lord promises a day of refining fire and a sudden coming to his temple. Before that coming, a messenger: “Behold I send my angel, and he shall prepare the way before my face. And presently the Lord, whom you seek, and the angel of the testament, whom you desire, shall come to his temple” (Malachi 3:1). Later the book promises Elijah’s return to turn the hearts of fathers and sons (Malachi 4:5–6).\n\nThe New Testament hears this without hesitation. Jesus asks the crowds what they went into the desert to see, and applies Malachi to John: this is he of whom it is written, Behold I send my angel before thy face (Matthew 11:10). Mark opens his Gospel with the voice preparing the way, blending Malachi and Isaiah (Mark 1:2–3). John’s identity is forerunner, not rival. He must decrease; the one who comes after is preferred before him.\n\nChristologically the oracle matters because the one whose way is prepared is the Lord coming to his temple. The messenger does not prepare for a mere rabbi. The “face” of the Lord approaches. When Jesus enters the temple and when John points to the Lamb of God, Malachi’s structure is enacted: preparation, then presence. The prophetic canon closes in expectation; the Gospel opens by naming the voice in the wilderness.\n\nThe pivotal moment teaches the Church the humility of secondary missions. Every authentic ministry prepares the way before his face, not its own. It also teaches expectancy: the Lord still comes — in Word, sacrament, and final judgment — and the Church’s voice is still that of a messenger, never the Bridegroom himself.\n\nLiturgically Advent keeps Malachi’s pressure alive: prepare the way, purify the sons of Levi, endure the day of his coming. John remains the model of Advent existence — voice, not Word; lamp, not Light; friend of the Bridegroom, not the Bridegroom. Every generation of the Church needs that clarity. When the messenger confuses himself with the Lord, or when the people seek the Lord without accepting the messenger’s call to repentance, Malachi’s warning still stands. The pivotal moment closes the Old Testament with an open road and a coming face — and the Gospel begins by filling both.",
