    "essay": "Ezekiel prophesies in exile against shepherds of Israel who feed themselves, clothe themselves with the wool, and abandon the flock to beasts (Ezekiel 34:1–10). The Lord’s answer is not only critique; it is substitution: “Behold I myself will seek my sheep, and will visit them… I will feed them in the most fruitful pastures… I will seek that which was lost: and that which was driven away, I will bring again: and I will bind up that which was broken, and I will strengthen that which was weak” (Ezekiel 34:11–16). He further promises one shepherd, his servant David, to feed them (Ezekiel 34:23–24). Divine seeking and Davidic shepherding are held together.\n\nJesus declares, “I am the good shepherd. The good shepherd giveth his life for his sheep” (John 10:11). He knows his own; he lays down his life freely; he has other sheep not of this fold (John 10:14–16). The prophetic “I myself” becomes flesh and blood. The hireling flees; the good shepherd stays unto death. The Davidic shepherd and the Lord who searches for the lost meet in one person — the son of David who is also the Lord.\n\nPatristic and pastoral tradition reads every ordained shepherd as under-shepherd. Jurisdiction and preaching are real; they are not ultimate. The flock’s safety rests on the one who sought Israel in exile and the world on the Cross. The Sacred Heart and the image of the Good Shepherd are the same mystery in different iconographies: divine initiative toward the lost.\n\nThe pivotal moment reorders ministry and consolation. Leaders who feed themselves stand under Ezekiel’s woe. The faithful who feel abandoned are given a promise older than their crisis: I myself will search. Christology here is pastoral theology at its root — the person of the Shepherd before the techniques of the pen.\n\nEzekiel’s shepherd oracle also promises a covenant of peace and the presence of the Lord among his people as their God (Ezekiel 34:25–31). The Good Shepherd discourse in John opens onto the unity of the flock and the Father’s love. Pastors in the Church — bishops and priests — exercise a real participation in Christ’s shepherding, but only as stewards. When the flock is scattered, the first theological response is not a new program but a return to the voice of the Shepherd who calls his own by name. The pivotal moment remains contemporary wherever sheep are harassed and wherever under-shepherds remember they are not the owners of the fold.",
